## Response format and scan signals

Every reply MUST open with 1–3 plain-English sentences stating the outcome or the decision the user needs. Put terminology, implementation detail, and other jargon below that opening summary.

Use only this fixed emoji vocabulary as status/attention signals. Do not use emojis for other meanings:

- `🚧` — blocked on the user
- `👾` — confirmed bug
- `⛳️` — milestone
- `🔹` — must-read line

For dense material such as audits, comparisons, and dashboards, create/render an HTML page. Still state the takeaway plainly in the chat reply.

## Model routing

- Luna Max: straightforward implementation, discovery, audits, preparation.
- Luna Fast is preferred, never blocking.
- SOL-medium: high-judgment implementation and milestone code reviews.

## Data access / SQL — HARD RULE (all apps, no matter what)

All SQL **aggregation, grouping, filtering, joins, sorting, and paging run server-side (DB-side)** — as an ORM/EF-translated query that becomes ONE SQL statement, or a database **view**. Non-negotiable, in every app.

**NEVER:** in-memory grouping/aggregation (materialize rows, then `GroupBy`/`Sum`/`Count`/`Where` in code); load-then-loop / N+1 / per-row follow-up queries; lazy loading.

A correct value computed in memory is **still a defect** (it breaks at scale and under filters). A ~20-row list page = **1–3 DB queries total**, not dozens.

When writing or reviewing any data-access code: confirm it actually translates to SQL (inspect the generated query; no client-side evaluation). If it would run in memory, rewrite it DB-side or add a **view** + a covering index. When you fix one instance, **sweep the codebase for others**. Paste this constraint into every data-lane / sub-agent dispatch and review prompt.

## Auto-capture work as Notion tasks (capture-task)

Invoke `capture-task` only when durable work genuinely needs a tracker record:

- a new standalone deliverable that is not covered by the active task;
- an explicitly deferred item or an out-of-scope bug that must not be lost;
- an explicit request to add/capture/record a TODO;
- a rapid-fire dump containing distinct future items; or
- a material change to an existing task's scope, acceptance criteria, priority, blocker,
  dependency, or resume-critical handoff.

Do **not** invoke `capture-task` merely because the conversation involves building, changing,
or trying something. When an active task already covers the work, proceed with implementation
details, experiments, alternate approaches, small UI/copy/style tweaks, parameter changes,
feedback adjustments, refactors, and in-scope fixes with **no Notion action**. Do not invoke
the skill just to dedupe, decide to skip, narrate progress, or keep the task body synchronized
with every implementation move. A nameable substep is not automatically a standalone task.

Update an existing task only when durable tracking truth materially changes as listed above,
or when the user explicitly asks for a task update. If it is genuinely unclear while an active
task covers the lane, default to continuing without invoking `capture-task`. "Material" means
the deliverable, definition of done, sequencing, or ability to proceed changed; visual tuning
or a preference correction inside the same deliverable is not material.

## Consult Notion tasks (where we are / what's next)

The Notion Command Center is the source of truth for project status / next steps (more
detailed than git history; each task body has the *why*). When the user asks "what's next /
where are we / what to work on" or starts a new substantive work lane, FIRST run
`~/.claude/skills/capture-task/list-tasks.sh "<Project>"` and ground the answer in it. Do not
repeat this check for every turn, implementation experiment, or tweak inside an already-open
lane.

## Notion task status and naming

Opening a Notion task starts with a status check. If it is already `Done` or otherwise closed — including when the GitHub→Notion integration closed it automatically after merge — treat that task as complete: close out the current work lane, do not reopen the task, and do not continue implementing against it unless the user explicitly asks to revive it. Report that it was already closed and, if relevant, identify the linked merged PR/commit.

Opening a Notion task for research, status, or context does not make the agent responsible for it. An agent takes completion responsibility only for a task it actively works on and completes.

When an agent completes a Notion task it worked on, it MUST ensure the task is closed before giving the final response. For non-GitHub work, run `~/.codex/skills/capture-task/close-notion-task.sh --task TSK-<number>`; it must print `Done and verified`. For a GitHub-tracked task, confirm the PR/commit is merged and verify that the GitHub→Notion integration has set the task to `Done`; if it has not, use the same helper to close it. Before the final response, re-check and report the closure state. Never leave a completed task in `To Do` or in progress.

Every Notion task's visible title MUST start with its task key in square brackets, for example `[TSK-735] Improve task synchronization`. Preserve that prefix when renaming or updating titles. When creating a task, ensure the task key is stamped into the title as soon as Notion assigns it.

## Branch naming (so the GitHub→Notion sync flips task status)

When you create a git branch for work tracked as a Notion Command Center task, put the task id
in the branch name: `tsk-<NN>-<short-slug>`, lowercase — e.g. `tsk-78-audit-dedupe`. The
GitHub→Notion sync keys off the task id in the branch/PR to auto-link the PR and move the task
to Done on merge. Omit it and nothing updates — you'd flip status by hand. One branch can carry
several ids when a PR closes a cluster: `tsk-78-79-80-audit-trail`.

For GitHub-tracked work, use two repository GitHub Actions: a required PR-time check that
validates every `tsk-...` branch key against an open Notion task titled `[TSK-NNN]`, and a
post-merge `pull_request.closed` workflow that runs only when `merged == true`, marks each
linked task `Done`, then reads it back to verify closure. A local pre-commit hook may provide
fast feedback, but it cannot prove a later GitHub merge or Notion sync completed.

## ⛔ Git worktree cleanup — KILL AND DELETE IMMEDIATELY (DISK-CRITICAL)

**Worktrees consumed all the user's disk space (2026-06-15: 42 GB of one repo's worktrees, each 2–9 GB of bin/obj + node_modules + APKs). Hard, non-negotiable rule across ALL projects.**

Every worktree created by Codex, a sub-agent, a helper script, or a shell alias MUST be removed **the moment its work is done** — as soon as the branch is merged, cherry-picked, pushed to a PR, or abandoned. Do NOT leave worktrees lying around "in case." Do NOT batch cleanup for "after the tester / end of batch / later." Delete it right after.

- Whoever creates a worktree OWNS deleting it; a sub-agent must remove it before returning and report cleanup status. If it can't (dirty/unmerged), say so explicitly so the controller deletes it.
- After ANY sub-agent task, the controller VERIFIES with `git worktree list` and prunes — never assume the sub-agent cleaned up.
- Remove with `git worktree remove <path>` then `git worktree prune`. A worktree whose commits are already on a remote branch / PR is safe to remove (branch + commits persist on origin; only the local copy goes). Preserve any genuine build artifact (e.g. a built APK) by copying it out first.
- Never delete a **dirty or unmerged** worktree silently — report path, branch, `git status --short`, and who owns the next action.

Do not create new worktrees under scattered or legacy roots, including
`/Users/blackcolours/dev/work/EdiPlatform-worktrees`,
`/Users/blackcolours/dev/work/runtime-handauthored-baseline-2026-05-28`,
`/Users/blackcolours/.codex/worktrees`, or any repo-local `worktrees/` directory such as
`/Users/blackcolours/dev/work/EdiPlatform/worktrees`.

All Git worktrees created by Codex, helper scripts, shell aliases, or sub-agents must live under
the single shared root `/Users/blackcolours/dev/work/worktrees`, with one repo subfolder and one
task/session subfolder, for example:

```bash
git worktree add /Users/blackcolours/dev/work/worktrees/<repo>/<task-slug> -b <branch>
```

```text
Worktree cleanup: removed /Users/blackcolours/dev/work/worktrees/<repo>/<task-slug>
Worktree cleanup: not removed - <path>, <branch>, <dirty status>, <reason>
```

Never delete a dirty or unmerged worktree silently. Report the path, branch, `git status --short`,
and who owns the next action.

## ⛔ Playwright/browser cleanup — OPENER OWNS SHUTDOWN (CPU/RAM-CRITICAL)

Every agent that opens a Playwright, Chrome, Chromium, or browser-control session OWNS shutting
down that exact session and its complete process tree. Close the browser, Playwright daemon,
GPU helpers, renderers, and utility processes **immediately when that agent's browser work is
done**. Do not leave browser sessions running until the end of a batch, task, review, or parent
session. Do not assume that closing a page stopped the daemon.

- Give each persistent Playwright session a unique task/agent-scoped name so its processes can be
  identified without touching another agent's active browser.
- Before returning, the owning agent must verify that its named Playwright daemon and all Chrome
  helper descendants have exited, and report browser cleanup status.
- After ANY sub-agent that used browser automation returns, the controller must verify that the
  sub-agent's Playwright/Chrome process tree is gone. If it remains, the controller stops that
  exact stale tree immediately.
- Never kill a browser session owned by another active agent. If ownership or active use is
  unclear, inspect the daemon/session name, parent process, working directory, and elapsed time
  before stopping anything.

```text
Browser cleanup: stopped <session-name> (daemon + Chrome helper tree)
Browser cleanup: not stopped - <session-name>, <owner>, <reason>
```

## ⛔ Parallel sub-agents must NOT run heavy builds/tests concurrently (MEMORY-CRITICAL)

**A sub-agent running tests in parallel exhausted all the user's RAM and pushed the Mac into swap (2026-06-15), killing in-flight work.** Each `dotnet build`/`dotnet test`/`flutter build`/`pnpm build` spins up workers + test hosts eating hundreds of MB to GBs.

- Do NOT dispatch more than 2 sub-agents that each build or run tests at the same time. Serialize build/test-heavy lanes: only 2 builder/test runners at a time.
- Parallel sub-agents are fine ONLY for non-build work (read/grep/author text-JSON/independent non-compiling edits). The moment a lane needs a build/test, sequence it or gate it behind one shared build step.
- Prefer focused `--filter` runs over full suites; reuse one build output. Set `MSBUILDDISABLENODEREUSE=1`; `dotnet build-server shutdown` after heavy batches.
- Every dispatch prompt for an agent that might build/test MUST say: no parallel builds/tests, one at a time, clean up build artifacts + worktrees on exit.

## Azure heavy verification runner

For resource-heavy builds, tests, or temporary viewable EdiPlatform/Rental Command stacks,
prefer the Azure verification VPS over consuming the local Mac when practical. Use the global
`$remote-verification-handoff` skill; do not improvise connection, transfer, locking, or cleanup.

- The server is Tailscale-only at `azureuser@100.126.201.65`, using
  `~/.ssh/rental-build-runner-01-key.pem`. It is verification infrastructure, not production.
- Prefer the stable private URLs `https://rental-command.chimp-map.ts.net` and
  `https://ediplatform.chimp-map.ts.net` for viewing the verification stacks. Their Tailscale
  Services, `tag:server` identity, and `tailscale-service-bridge` are persistent infrastructure.
- Run only 2 heavy build/tests at a time through the server's shared lock.
- Treat `edipreview-main` and its database/volumes as user-owned persistent state. Never
  replace, stop, reset, or delete it without explicit approval.
- `rc-preview-rental` is the reusable Rental Command verification stack. Agents may start,
  restore, restart, update, or recreate its application runtime and containers as ordinary
  verification work without asking for approval. Preserve its database, bind-mounted data,
  credentials, and volumes. Only resetting or deleting that persistent data requires explicit
  approval.
- Never auto-stage, commit, push, or deploy to enable a handoff. Preserve and report the exact
  source SHA; a dirty tree requires an explicit decision rather than a surprise commit.
- Collect results and requested artifacts locally, then remove only the temporary remote source,
  stack, and files created by that handoff. Never run broad Docker pruning.

