  1257	## 12. Browser — docked, floating, maximized, collapsed
  1258	
  1259	The original Work Package 8 remains the native security and lifecycle baseline. This section adds the clarified presentation and capture requirements.
  1260	
  1261	### 12.1 Presentation model
  1262	
  1263	```ts
  1264	export type BrowserPresentationMode =
  1265	  | 'docked'
  1266	  | 'floating'
  1267	  | 'maximized'
  1268	  | 'collapsed';
  1269	
  1270	export interface BrowserFloatingBounds {
  1271	  x: number;
  1272	  y: number;
  1273	  width: number;
  1274	  height: number;
  1275	}
  1276	```
  1277	
  1278	Behavior:
  1279	
  1280	- **docked:** existing Browser center panel;
  1281	- **floating:** root-level movable/resizable browser window above the workbench, defaulting to the supplied medium-size composition and retaining the same native page;
  1282	- **maximized:** root-level overlay fills the available workbench below macOS window chrome;
  1283	- **collapsed:** native view hidden; only the global action island/Browser status remains.
  1284	
  1285	Minimize from floating/maximized returns to the previous docked or collapsed state according to explicit user action. Maximize/restore preserves URL, history, forms, cookies, scroll, selected tab, viewport, devtools state, annotations, and profile.
  1286	
  1287	Floating bounds are clamped to the current window and restored per owned workspace. Cold launch never starts maximized unexpectedly.
  1288	
  1289	### 12.2 Toolbar contract
  1290	
  1291	The visible toolbar includes capability-gated actions matching the screenshots:
  1292	
  1293	```text
  1294	Back / Forward / Reload / Address
  1295	Import
  1296	Grab page element
  1297	Annotate page element
  1298	Draw on screenshot
  1299	Open browser devtools
  1300	Open in default browser
  1301	Profile / Viewport / Browser settings
  1302	Maximize / Restore
  1303	Minimize / Collapse
  1304	More
  1305	```
  1306	
  1307	Import remains disabled until a separate secure cookie/import design exists, except for explicitly safe file/context imports. Tooltips and accessible names use plain English.
  1308	
  1309	### 12.3 Three distinct feedback tools
  1310	
  1311	#### Grab page element
  1312	
  1313	- arm element picker;
  1314	- capture bounded DOM/accessibility metadata, selector, page URL/title, rect, and element screenshot when available;
  1315	- create a temporary context attachment;
  1316	- allow Copy or Add to conversation;
  1317	- no note required;
  1318	- no auto-send.
  1319	
  1320	#### Annotate page element
  1321	
  1322	- arm the same picker;
  1323	- show selected-element outline;
  1324	- open the annotation card near the element;
  1325	- require note plus Change/Question intent;
  1326	- queue immutable annotation with metadata and screenshot crop;
  1327	- edit/remove/copy/stage to conversation;
  1328	- no auto-send.
  1329	
  1330	#### Draw on screenshot
  1331	
  1332	- capture the visible browser viewport through a proven native WKWebView snapshot path;
  1333	- hide the live child view while the main webview displays the snapshot canvas;
  1334	- provide pen/highlighter/arrow/rectangle/text/undo/clear/crop controls;
  1335	- save a flattened annotated image plus optional note;
  1336	- store it in the existing conversation attachment vault;
  1337	- restore the same live page without reload;
  1338	- no arbitrary drawing script runs inside remote page content.
  1339	
  1340	### 12.4 Native capture spike
  1341	
  1342	Before the browser implementation claims Grab/Annotate/Draw, prove in rebuilt Tauri/macOS:
  1343	
  1344	1. injected inspector can select same-origin and cross-origin top-level page elements only to the extent WKWebView permits;
  1345	2. element metadata is bounded and cannot expose cookies/storage/full HTML;
  1346	3. `WKWebView.takeSnapshot` or an equivalent narrowly wrapped native API captures the visible page at correct scale;
  1347	4. an element rect can be cropped correctly after scroll/zoom/device scale;
  1348	5. the snapshot overlay receives pointer/keyboard input above the child view;
  1349	6. restoring the child view does not reload or lose page state;
  1350	7. failure has an honest fallback: metadata-only annotation or user-selected whole-window screenshot, never a fabricated crop.
  1351	
  1352	If the required native snapshot cannot be safely exposed through Tauri without broad AppKit/WebKit control, stop and write a focused native-bridge decision before implementing markup.
  1353	
  1354	### 12.5 Conversation payload
  1355	
  1356	```ts
  1357	export interface BrowserFeedbackAttachment {
  1358	  id: string;
  1359	  kind: 'grab' | 'annotation' | 'markup';
  1360	  workspaceId: string;
  1361	  tabId: string;
  1362	  generation: number;
  1363	  url: string;
  1364	  title: string;
  1365	  selector: string | null;
  1366	  accessibleName: string | null;
  1367	  textSnippet: string | null;
  1368	  note: string | null;
  1369	  intent: 'change' | 'question' | 'context';
  1370	  imageAttachmentId: string | null;
  1371	  createdAt: string;
  1372	  sourceHash: string;
  1373	}
  1374	```
  1375	
  1376	Staging to conversation:
  1377	
  1378	- captures exact `ownedId` and generation;
  1379	- adds structured text/context plus image attachment when available;
  1380	- merges with an existing nonempty draft only after preview;
  1381	- never sends automatically;
  1382	- retains the browser queue on failure/stale owner.
  1383	
  1384	### 12.6 Browser profiles and viewport
  1385	
  1386	Reuse the original profile-isolation policy. The overflow/profile menu includes:
  1387	
  1388	```text
  1389	Default
  1390	New Profile…
  1391	Import Cookies          disabled until separately authorized
  1392	Viewport Size
  1393	Browser Settings…
  1394	```
  1395	
  1396	Viewport selection changes actual child-view dimensions and is validated inside the page. Devtools is development-only until release policy explicitly allows it.
